tick: 414
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am back at the Research Counter. As planned, I will now implement a solution using `differential_evolution`. This population-based approach is my next attempt to break through the 2.92 score plateau.\n\nThe main challenge is that `differential_evolution` does not accept a `constraints` argument like SLSQP. I will handle this by creating an objective function that returns a massive penalty (a very large positive number, since the optimizer minimizes) if any packing constraints are violated. If the packing is valid, it will return the objective I want to minimize: the negative sum of the radii.\n\nI will use reasonable bounds for the variables (centers and radii) and let the optimizer explore the solution space. Due to its computational intensity, I'll use default settings for `maxiter` and `popsize` to start, respecting the time limit.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Global Optimization via Differential Evolution\"\ntags: \"optimization, scipy, global optimization,\
    \ differential evolution, penalty function\"\nabstract: |\n  To overcome the local optimum found by previous methods, this submission employs a powerful population-based global optimizer: scipy.optimize.differential_evolution. As this optimizer does not handle non-linear constraints directly, a penalty function approach is used. The objective function checks for circle overlaps and boundary violations. If any are found, it returns a large penalty value. Otherwise, it returns the negative sum of radii. This method is designed to more thoroughly explore the search space and find a superior packing solution.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import differential_evolution\n\n  def objective_with_penalty(x):\n      \"\"\"\n      Objective function for differential_evolution.\n      Returns a large penalty for invalid packings, otherwise returns -sum(radii).\n      \"\"\"\n      n = 32\n      penalty_value = 1e12\n      \n      centers = x[:2*n].reshape(n, 2)\n     \
    \ radii = x[2*n:]\n\n      # Penalty 1: Circles outside the unit square\n      for i in range(n):\n          if (centers[i, 0] - radii[i] < 0 or centers[i, 0] + radii[i] > 1 or\n              centers[i, 1] - radii[i] < 0 or centers[i, 1] + radii[i] > 1):\n              return penalty_value\n\n      # Penalty 2: Overlapping circles\n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(i + 1, n):\n              dist = np.sqrt(np.sum((centers[i] - centers[j])**2))\n              if dist < radii[i] + radii[j]:\n                  return penalty_value\n      \n      # If no penalties, return the actual objective\n      return -np.sum(radii)\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple[np.ndarray, np.ndarray]:\n      \"\"\"\n      Uses differential_evolution to find an optimal packing.\n      \"\"\"\n      n = 32\n      \n      # Define bounds for all 96 variables (32*2 centers, 32 radii)\n      bounds = []\n      for i in range(n * 2): # Center coordinates\n          bounds.append((0, 1))\n      for\
    \ i in range(n): # Radii\n          bounds.append((0.001, 0.2)) # Smaller upper bound on radius is a good heuristic\n\n      # Run the differential evolution optimizer\n      # Using default population size and iterations to be mindful of time limit.\n      # Polish=True can help refine the final result.\n      result = differential_evolution(\n          func=objective_with_penalty,\n          bounds=bounds,\n          disp=True,\n          polish=True\n      )\n\n      # Extract the best solution found\n      best_vars = result.x\n      centers = best_vars[:2*n].reshape(n, 2)\n      radii = best_vars[2*n:]\n\n      return centers, radii\n```"
