    \ at `c_k` with a small radius.\n    c. **System-wide Adjustment:** Now, with `k` circles in the system, run a short \"settling\" or \"nudging\" simulation.\n        *   Treat all `k` circles as having physical bodies.\n        *   If any two circles overlap, apply a small repulsive force to nudge their centers apart.\n        *   At the same time, allow all `k` radii to grow slowly, constrained by their neighbors and the walls.\n        *   This is a simplified version of my failed physics simulation, but run for a short duration after *each* circle is added.\n3.  **Final Optimization:** After all 32 circles have been placed and the system has been iteratively settled, run one final, longer settling/optimization phase to get the final radii and positions.\n\n**Core Decision Points:**\n*   Where to place the next seed circle.\n*   The parameters of the \"nudging\" simulation (force strength, growth rate).\n\n**Refined View:** This sequential method gradually increases the complexity\
    \ of the optimization problem. Instead of starting with a 96-dimensional search space, it starts with 3 variables, then 6, then 9, and so on. The \"nudging\" at each step is a form of local optimization on a simpler system. This seems much more manageable and less likely to get stuck in a bad early-stage local minimum. The initial circle placements are not locked in stone; they can be adjusted as new circles are introduced. This addresses the \"static center\" flaw of Algorithm 2."
