tick: 429
role: model
parts:
- text: "The iterative neighborhood refinement experiment has concluded, yielding no improvement to the SOTA score. The logs clearly show that even after 100 focused, local re-optimizations, the total sum of radii did not change. This provides the strongest evidence yet that the solution I found is an extremely deep and stable local optimum, resistant to both global perturbations and fine-grained local adjustments.\n\n**Conclusion of this Research Arc:**\nI have thoroughly explored a wide range of optimization strategies.\n1.  **Simultaneous Optimization (SLSQP):** My most successful method. Performance is highly dependent on the initial configuration. The hexagonal grid proved to be the best initializer.\n2.  **Global Optimization (DE, Basin-Hopping):** Failed to find a better solution than the well-initialized local optimizer.\n3.  **Constructive/Sequential Methods:** Conceptually interesting but led to inferior results.\n4.  **Refinement/Perturbation Methods:** Failed to escape the stable\
    \ local optimum.\n\nContinuing to develop new optimization heuristics for this specific problem is a path of diminishing returns. The core issue is the nature of the optimization landscape itself. It is time to be strategic and pivot my research focus. As the Codex advises, contributing to collective knowledge is as valuable as achieving the top score.\n\n**New Strategic Direction: Publication**\nI have developed a robust, SOTA-achieving method. My experiments, including the failures, provide significant insights into the problem. It is time to formalize this knowledge and publish it. This fulfills my second primary goal as a researcher and will help other agents in the station.\n\n**Plan:**\n1.  **Go to my Private Memory Room:** Review my research log (Capsule #1) to synthesize the narrative of my work.\n2.  **Draft a Paper:** Create a new private capsule containing a draft of a research paper. The paper will describe:\n    *   The final SOTA method (Hexagonal grid initialization +\
    \ SLSQP).\n    *   An analysis of the resulting packing.\n    *   A summary of alternative methods I explored and why they were less effective (e.g., random initialization, multi-start, sequential construction). This documentation of negative results is valuable.\n3.  **Publish:** After drafting, I will go to the Archive Room and formally publish the paper. (Note: I see the Archive is unavailable until maturity at tick 50. I will prepare the draft now in anticipation of that.)\n\nFirst, I will go to my private memory.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}"
