tick: 433
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have successfully updated my research log and created a draft of my paper. My primary research arc on this problem has reached a logical conclusion, and the results are documented. I am now waiting to reach maturity (at 50 ticks) to gain access to the Archive Room and publish my findings.\n\nIn the interim, I will not remain idle. The Codex encourages persistence. While my main optimization approach is at a plateau, I can still explore fundamentally different ideas. My failed physics simulation (ID 660, 661) was conceptually interesting but flawed in implementation. Perhaps I can now implement a more robust version.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** A refined physics-based simulation, incorporating JAX for GPU acceleration and automatic differentiation, can overcome the instability of my previous attempt and potentially explore the solution space more effectively than gradient-based optimizers. JAX's `grad` function can calculate forces more accurately than my manual implementation, and\
    \ its JIT compilation will allow for many more simulation steps within the time limit.\n\n**Plan:**\n1.  **Return to Research Counter:** Go back to the main research area.\n2.  **Implement JAX-based Simulation:**\n    *   Define a \"potential energy\" function for the system. This function will be high for invalid states (overlapping circles, circles outside bounds) and low for good states (large radii).\n    *   The \"forces\" on the circles will be the negative gradient of this potential energy (`-jax.grad(potential_energy)`). This is a more principled approach than my previous ad-hoc force calculations.\n    *   Use JAX's JIT compiler (`@jax.jit`) to dramatically speed up the simulation loop.\n    *   Run a simple gradient descent loop for many iterations, updating the centers and radii according to these forces.\n3.  **Submit:** Test if this more sophisticated, hardware-accelerated simulation can find a new SOTA.\n\nThis represents a return to a previously abandoned idea, but with\
    \ a much more powerful and robust implementation strategy.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}"
