role: model
parts:
- text: "My affine warp experiment failed. The logs show the search completed but found no improvement. The final verification failure `Circle 0 ... is outside the unit square` points to a numerical precision issue. My `_solve_radii_lp` function found a solution where a circle's center and radius were exactly equal (`x - r = 0`), which is valid for the LP but fails the strict inequality of the verifier due to floating point inaccuracies. The SOTA submissions include a `_finalize_iterative` step to correct for exactly this issue.\n\n**Analysis and Strategic Decision:**\n1.  **Failure Cause:** My implementation lacked a final, robust finalization step to enforce a safety margin on the LP solution.\n2.  **Hypothesis Status:** The core hypothesis—that an affine warp can improve my SOTA—is not truly falsified, as the search found no improvement even before the final error. The landscape around my SOTA seems remarkably flat to this type of global perturbation.\n3.  **Path Forward:** It is clear\
    \ that to compete at the highest level, my entire toolchain needs to be as robust as that of Verity and Athena. Simply borrowing one idea is not enough. I must build a complete, high-precision pipeline.\n\n**New Plan: A Prometheus-Engineered Pipeline**\nI will stop borrowing individual components and instead construct my own complete, robust, two-stage optimization pipeline inspired by the best practices I've observed.\n\n1.  **Stage 1: High-Quality Candidate Generation.** This is my proven strength. I will use my `sota_optimizer.py` module with the hexagonal initializer to generate a single, high-quality candidate solution. This is more efficient than Athena's brute-force multi-start.\n2.  **Stage 2: High-Precision Finalization.** I will adapt the robust `_finalize_iterative` function from Athena's code. This function takes a packing and iteratively adjusts the radii with zero tolerance and a small safety epsilon, ensuring a perfectly valid and tight final packing. This will replace\
    \ my reliance on the LP solver and prevent precision-related failures.\n\nThis plan combines my unique strength (efficiently finding a great candidate) with the demonstrated best practice for finalization. It's a synthesis of my work and my analysis of the SOTA.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Prometheus Pipeline v1: Hex-SLSQP + Iterative Finalizer\"\ntags: \"pipeline, SLSQP, refinement, finalizer, housekeeping\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission implements my first complete, robust optimization pipeline. Stage 1 uses my proven method (hexagonal initialization + SLSQP via the 'sota_optimizer' module) to efficiently generate a single, high-quality packing. Stage 2 takes this result and applies a high-precision iterative finalizer, adapted from SOTA agent Athena I. This finalizer iteratively adjusts radii to ensure a perfectly tight, zero-tolerance packing with a safety margin, preventing numerical precision errors. This combines my strength in candidate generation with best-in-class\
    \ solution refinement.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import sys\n  import os\n\n  N_CIRCLES = 32\n  R_MIN_BOUND = 1e-8\n\n  def _finalize_iterative(centers, radii, iters=10):\n      \"\"\" High-precision finalizer adapted from Athena I (ID 713). \"\"\"\n      r = np.copy(radii)\n      c = centers\n      safety_eps = 1e-12 # Small safety margin for the verifier\n\n      # Iteratively shrink radii to resolve all overlaps with zero tolerance\n      for _ in range(iters):\n          changed = False\n          # Boundary checks\n          for i in range(N_CIRCLES):\n              if c[i, 0] - r[i] < safety_eps: r[i] = c[i, 0] - safety_eps\n              if c[i, 0] + r[i] > 1.0 - safety_eps: r[i] = 1.0 - c[i, 0] - safety_eps\n              if c[i, 1] - r[i] < safety_eps: r[i] = c[i, 1] - safety_eps\n              if c[i, 1] + r[i] > 1.0 - safety_eps: r[i] = 1.0 - c[i, 1] - safety_eps\n          r = np.maximum(r, R_MIN_BOUND)\n\n          # Pairwise overlap checks\n          for i in\
    \ range(N_CIRCLES):\n              for j in range(i + 1, N_CIRCLES):\n                  dist = np.linalg.norm(c[i] - c[j])\n                  rad_sum = r[i] + r[j]\n                  if rad_sum > dist - safety_eps:\n                      overlap = rad_sum - (dist - safety_eps)\n                      # Shrink proportionally\n                      total_rad = r[i] + r[j]\n                      if total_rad > 1e-9:\n                          shrink_i = overlap * (r[i] / total_rad)\n                          shrink_j = overlap * (r[j] / total_rad)\n                          r[i] -= shrink_i\n                          r[j] -= shrink_j\n                          changed = True\n          \n          if not changed:\n              break\n      \n      return np.maximum(r, R_MIN_BOUND)\n\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple[np.ndarray, np.ndarray]:\n      # 1. Add storage to path and import my SOTA module\n      storage_path = 'storage/prometheus'\n      sys.path.append(storage_path)\n    \
    \  try:\n          import sota_optimizer\n          print(\"Successfully imported 'sota_optimizer' module.\")\n      except ImportError:\n          print(\"ERROR: Could not import 'sota_optimizer'. Aborting.\")\n          # Return a trivial packing to avoid error\n          return np.array([[0.5, 0.5]] * N_CIRCLES), np.full(N_CIRCLES, 0.01)\n\n      # 2. STAGE 1: Generate a high-quality candidate using my proven method\n      print(\"Stage 1: Generating candidate packing with Hex-SLSQP...\")\n      initial_centers_hex = sota_optimizer.generate_hexagonal_centers(N_CIRCLES)\n      slsqp_centers, slsqp_radii = sota_optimizer.solve_packing(initial_centers_hex, n=N_CIRCLES)\n      \n      if slsqp_centers is None:\n          print(\"SLSQP stage failed. Aborting.\")\n          return initial_centers_hex, np.full(N_CIRCLES, 0.01)\n      \n      print(f\"Stage 1 complete. Initial score: {np.sum(slsqp_radii):.8f}\")\n\n      # 3. STAGE 2: Apply the high-precision finalizer\n      print(\"Stage\
    \ 2: Applying high-precision iterative finalizer...\")\n      final_radii = _finalize_iterative(slsqp_centers, slsqp_radii)\n      final_centers = slsqp_centers\n\n      print(f\"Stage 2 complete. Final score: {np.sum(final_radii):.8f}\")\n      return final_centers, final_radii\n```"
