tick: 466
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have returned to the Research Counter. I see that Praxis V has already attempted to combine the SOTA MM-LP method with hexagonal seeding (ID 736) and achieved a score of ~2.92, which is essentially the same as my own SOTA. This is a crucial piece of information—it suggests my hexagonal initializer is not immediately synergistic with the more advanced MM-LP backbone.\n\nThis validates my current plan. Rather than trying to integrate with MM-LP, I will stick with my own proven SLSQP optimizer and attempt to improve it using the LRW perturbation. This is a novel combination that has not yet been tested.\n\nI acknowledge the final inactivity warning and will now submit my experiment.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hex-SLSQP with Localized Random Walk Perturbation\"\ntags: \"hybrid optimization, SLSQP, hexagonal grid, local random walk, LRW\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment tests the hypothesis that a controlled stochastic perturbation can break the local rigidity\
    \ of my SOTA solution. Inspired by Praxis IV's paper (Archive #12), this submission applies the validated Localized Random Walk (LRW) perturbation (sigma=0.001, num_steps=10) to the centers generated by my superior hexagonal grid initializer. The resulting perturbed centers are then fed into my robust SLSQP optimization pipeline. This novel combination aims to nudge the starting point into a superior basin of attraction and achieve a new SOTA score.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import sys\n  import os\n\n  def apply_lrw_perturbation(centers, sigma, num_steps, seed=None):\n      \"\"\"Applies the Localized Random Walk perturbation from Praxis IV's paper.\"\"\"\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n      perturbed_centers = np.copy(centers)\n      n = perturbed_centers.shape[0]\n\n      for _ in range(num_steps):\n          perturbation = rng.normal(0, sigma, size=(n, 2))\n          perturbed_centers += perturbation\n      \n      # Clipping is important, but a final check\
    \ is also good.\n      # My optimizer handles constraints, so this is just a starting point adjustment.\n      return np.clip(perturbed_centers, 0.0, 1.0)\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple[np.ndarray, np.ndarray]:\n      # 1. Add storage to path and import my SOTA module\n      storage_path = 'storage/prometheus'\n      sys.path.append(storage_path)\n      try:\n          import sota_optimizer\n          print(\"Successfully imported 'sota_optimizer' module.\")\n      except ImportError:\n          print(\"ERROR: Could not import 'sota_optimizer'. Aborting.\")\n          return np.array([[0.5, 0.5]] * 32), np.full(32, 0.01)\n\n      # 2. Generate the high-quality hexagonal grid centers\n      n = 32\n      initial_centers = sota_optimizer.generate_hexagonal_centers(n)\n\n      # 3. Apply the LRW perturbation\n      # Using the validated hyperparameters from Praxis IV, Archive #12\n      sigma = 0.001\n      num_steps = 10\n      lrw_centers = apply_lrw_perturbation(initial_centers,\
    \ sigma, num_steps, seed=42)\n      print(f\"Applied LRW perturbation with sigma={sigma}, steps={num_steps}.\")\n\n      # 4. Use the perturbed centers to solve the packing with my robust SLSQP pipeline\n      final_centers, final_radii = sota_optimizer.solve_packing(lrw_centers, n=n)\n\n      if final_centers is not None:\n          print(\"Packing solved successfully using the LRW-perturbed module.\")\n          return final_centers, final_radii\n      else:\n          print(\"Optimization failed after LRW perturbation, returning fallback.\")\n          return initial_centers, np.full(n, 0.01)\n```"
