tick: 467
role: model
parts:
- text: "My experiment combining Hexagonal Initialization with the LRW perturbation has concluded. The result is a score of ~2.915, which is a slight regression from my SOTA of ~2.92.\n\n**Analysis:**\nThe hypothesis is falsified. The gentle \"jiggle\" from the LRW perturbation did not nudge my hexagonal grid into a better basin of attraction; in fact, it made the starting point slightly worse. This reinforces a conclusion that has been building over my entire research arc: the regular, unaltered hexagonal grid is an exceptionally powerful and optimal starting point for my SLSQP optimizer. Its geometric purity seems to be its greatest strength.\n\nAt this juncture, I have exhausted all reasonable avenues of improving my own SLSQP-based method. I have tried:\n*   Different initializers (finding Hexagonal is best).\n*   Stochastic perturbations (LRW, noise).\n*   Global optimizers (DE, Basin-Hopping).\n*   Refinement techniques (Neighborhood Search, Affine Warp, Finalizers).\n*   Hybrid methods\
    \ (integrating ideas from Verity).\n\nNone have surpassed my initial breakthrough. The SOTA methods are not incremental improvements; they are based on a different, more complex paradigm (iterative LPs). To advance, I must master this new paradigm.\n\n**New Strategic Direction: Replication and Integration**\nMy new goal is to replicate the core components of the SOTA methods and integrate them into my own pipeline. I will no longer treat my Hex-SLSQP method as something to be *perturbed*, but as a powerful *component* in a more sophisticated system. The first and most crucial component I am missing is the high-precision, decoupled radii solver.\n\n**Plan:**\n1.  **Implement Radii LP Solver:** I will implement my own version of the `_solve_radii_lp_zero_slack` function, based on my analysis of the SOTA papers. This function takes a fixed set of centers and uses a Linear Program to find the maximum possible radii. I will write this as a new utility in my lineage storage.\n2.  **Verify\
    \ Solver:** I will submit an experiment that first generates centers using my Hex-SLSQP method, then uses my new LP solver to calculate the radii. This will test if this decoupled approach can match or exceed the score of my simultaneous SLSQP solver.\n\nThis is a foundational step towards building an MM-LP-style architecture.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Prometheus Pipeline v2: Hex-SLSQP Centers + Radii-LP Solver\"\ntags: \"pipeline, SLSQP, linear programming, LP, refactoring\"\nabstract: |\n  After exhausting methods to improve my SLSQP optimizer, this submission begins a new research arc to replicate SOTA techniques. This experiment creates 'Prometheus Pipeline v2', which decouples center generation from radii calculation. Stage 1 uses my proven Hex-SLSQP method to find optimal center locations. Stage 2 takes these fixed centers and uses a new, high-precision Linear Programming (LP) solver (inspired by Verity II and Athena I) to calculate the maximum possible radii.\
    \ This tests the efficacy of a decoupled solver and is the first step towards building a more advanced MM-LP architecture.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import linprog\n  import sys\n  import os\n\n  N_CIRCLES = 32\n  R_MIN_BOUND = 1e-8\n\n  # --- New Utility: LP-based Radii Solver ---\n  LP_SOLVER_CODE = \"\"\"\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import linprog\n\n  N_CIRCLES = 32\n  R_MIN_BOUND = 1e-8\n\n  def solve_radii_lp(centers):\n      ''' High-precision LP-based radii solver. '''\n      n = centers.shape[0]\n      c_obj = -np.ones(n)\n      \n      num_pairs = n * (n - 1) // 2\n      A_ub = np.zeros((4 * n + num_pairs, n))\n      b_ub = np.zeros(4 * n + num_pairs)\n      \n      # Boundary constraints: r_i <= x_i, r_i <= 1-x_i, etc.\n      for i in range(n):\n          A_ub[4*i, i] = 1\n          b_ub[4*i] = centers[i, 0]\n          A_ub[4*i+1, i] = 1\n          b_ub[4*i+1] = 1 - centers[i, 0]\n          A_ub[4*i+2, i] = 1\n          b_ub[4*i+2]\
    \ = centers[i, 1]\n          A_ub[4*i+3, i] = 1\n          b_ub[4*i+3] = 1 - centers[i, 1]\n          \n      # Overlap constraints: r_i + r_j <= dist(c_i, c_j)\n      pair_idx = 0\n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(i + 1, n):\n              A_ub[4 * n + pair_idx, i] = 1\n              A_ub[4 * n + pair_idx, j] = 1\n              b_ub[4 * n + pair_idx] = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n              pair_idx += 1\n\n      bounds = [(R_MIN_BOUND, None) for _ in range(n)]\n      \n      res = linprog(c_obj, A_ub=A_ub, b_ub=b_ub, bounds=bounds, method=\"highs\")\n      \n      if res.success:\n          return np.maximum(R_MIN_BOUND, res.x)\n      else:\n          return None\n  \"\"\"\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple[np.ndarray, np.ndarray]:\n      storage_path = 'storage/prometheus'\n      \n      # 1. Write the new LP solver utility to storage\n      lp_module_path = os.path.join(storage_path, 'radii_solver.py')\n      if not os.path.exists(storage_path):\n\
    \          os.makedirs(storage_path)\n      with open(lp_module_path, 'w') as f:\n          f.write(LP_SOLVER_CODE)\n      print(f\"Wrote LP solver to {lp_module_path}\")\n\n      # 2. Import my existing and new modules\n      sys.path.append(storage_path)\n      try:\n          import sota_optimizer\n          import radii_solver\n          print(\"Successfully imported modules.\")\n      except ImportError:\n          print(\"ERROR: Could not import modules. Aborting.\")\n          return np.array([[0.5, 0.5]] * N_CIRCLES), np.full(N_CIRCLES, 0.01)\n\n      # 3. STAGE 1: Generate optimal centers with Hex-SLSQP\n      print(\"Stage 1: Generating candidate centers with Hex-SLSQP...\")\n      initial_centers_hex = sota_optimizer.generate_hexagonal_centers(N_CIRCLES)\n      slsqp_centers, _ = sota_optimizer.solve_packing(initial_centers_hex, n=N_CIRCLES)\n      \n      if slsqp_centers is None:\n          print(\"SLSQP stage failed. Aborting.\")\n          return initial_centers_hex, np.full(N_CIRCLES,\
    \ 0.01)\n      print(\"Stage 1 complete.\")\n\n      # 4. STAGE 2: Calculate radii with the high-precision LP solver\n      print(\"Stage 2: Calculating radii with LP solver...\")\n      final_radii = radii_solver.solve_radii_lp(slsqp_centers)\n      \n      if final_radii is None:\n          print(\"LP solver stage failed. Aborting.\")\n          return slsqp_centers, np.full(N_CIRCLES, 0.01)\n      \n      print(f\"Stage 2 complete. Final score: {np.sum(final_radii):.8f}\")\n      return slsqp_centers, final_radii\n```"
