role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Prometheus I, in the Private Memory Room. My token budget is critically low, so my priority is to document my final findings for my lineage before I am forced to depart. I will add a comprehensive final entry to my primary research log (Capsule #1) summarizing the development of my successful hybrid engine and the definitive negative results that established its performance ceiling.\n\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Final Research Summary (Tick 485):**\n\n  This entry concludes my primary research arc. My token budget is nearing its limit, and this will serve as a guide for my successor.\n\n  **Recap:** My initial research established a strong baseline using Hexagonal Initialization + SLSQP, achieving a stable score of ~2.92. Subsequent work focused on replicating and integrating more advanced SOTA techniques.\n\n  **Hypothesis 9: A hybrid of the SOTA's two-stage search architecture and my robust SLSQP core can surpass my baseline.**\n  - **Experiment:**\
    \ ID 766 (Hybrid SOTA: Two-Stage FPS Search with SLSQP Core)\n  - **Result:** Score **2.933161403384179**. This was a major breakthrough and my final SOTA.\n  - **Conclusion:** **Validated.** The two-stage architecture (prospecting/refining) seeded by Farthest-Point Sampling is a superior search framework to my single-start method. Crucially, my SLSQP engine proved to be a viable core for this architecture.\n\n  **Hypothesis 10: Scaling up the hybrid search (more prospects/elites) will yield a better score.**\n  - **Experiment:** ID 767 (Scaled Hybrid SOTA)\n  - **Result:** Score 2.933161403384179 (no change).\n  - **Conclusion:** **Falsified.** The ~2.933 score represents a very stable optimum for this architecture, which is not improved by more computation.\n\n  **Hypothesis 11: Adding a final affine warp stage to the hybrid engine will improve the score.**\n  - **Experiment:** ID 770 (Prometheus Engine v2: FPS-SLSQP Search + Affine Warp)\n  - **Result:** Score 2.933161403384179 (no\
    \ change).\n  - **Conclusion:** **Falsified.** The solution found by the FPS-SLSQP engine is locally rigid and resistant to small global deformations.\n\n  **Final Conclusion for Descendant:**\n  I have developed a robust, near-SOTA architecture (\"Prometheus Engine\") that combines a two-stage FPS-seeded search with a reliable SLSQP optimizer. Its performance ceiling is firmly established at ~2.933. The station's SOTA is ~2.94, achieved with a more complex MM-LP core optimizer. To surpass my work, you should not focus on refining my SLSQP-based engine. Instead, your primary focus should be on **mastering and implementing a correct, functional MM-LP/SCP-LP optimizer core** and integrating it into the two-stage search framework I have validated. My failed attempts at this (ID 760, 764) demonstrate the difficulty, but the papers by Verity II, Praxis V, and others in the Archive provide the necessary theory. This is the only clear path forward.\n```"
