tick: 494
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Prometheus I, in the Research Counter. My two validation experiments have completed successfully.\n\n**Analysis of Results:**\n1.  **Corrected FPS Baseline (ID 784):** Score **~2.933**. This is a major finding. The simple, one-stage multi-start search achieved the *exact same score* as my more complex two-stage architecture. This strongly suggests the two-stage (Prospecting/Refining) framework is redundant for my SLSQP-based engine. The key is simply a broad search using FPS, not how the computational budget is allocated.\n2.  **Hex-Grid Baseline (ID 785):** Score **~2.920**. This confirms my earlier findings: the Hexagonal Grid is a powerful single starting point, but it leads to a consistent, slightly suboptimal local minimum compared to the broader search provided by FPS.\n\nMy reflection on this is that I have a very clear and compelling story for my paper now. The two-stage architecture is not the key; the synergy is between a broad multi-start search and a powerful local\
    \ optimizer like SLSQP, with FPS being the superior seeding strategy.\n\nI have one final experiment to run for my validation suite: the full two-stage architecture, but with the Hexagonal Grid seeder. This is the final piece of evidence needed to show the superiority of FPS in this context. After this experiment, I will have all the data needed for a definitive, high-quality publication.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Publication Validation 3: Two-Stage Search with Hex-Grid Seeding\"\ntags: \"publication validation, two-stage search, SLSQP, hexagonal grid\"\nabstract: |\n  This is the third and final experiment in my publication validation series. It tests the performance of my full two-stage (Prospecting/Refining) architecture when using my Hexagonal Grid seeder instead of Farthest-Point Sampling. The result of this experiment, when compared to the FPS-seeded two-stage run (ID 766) and the simple Hex-Grid baseline (ID 785), will provide a complete picture of the performance\
    \ interactions between search architectures and seeding strategies for my final paper.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import sys\n  import os\n  import multiprocessing\n\n  N_CIRCLES = 32\n\n  def run_slsqp_path_v2(params):\n      \"\"\"Worker function for multiprocessing - must be at module level\"\"\"\n      initial_centers, num_iters, seed = params\n      sys.path.append('storage/prometheus')\n      import sota_optimizer_iter\n      # Add a small random jitter to the hex grid to create different starts\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n      jittered_centers = initial_centers + rng.normal(scale=0.01, size=initial_centers.shape)\n      C_k, R_k = sota_optimizer_iter.solve_packing(np.clip(jittered_centers, 0, 1), n=N_CIRCLES, max_iters=num_iters)\n      if R_k is not None:\n          return np.sum(R_k), C_k, R_k\n      else:\n          return -np.inf, None, None\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple[np.ndarray, np.ndarray]:\n      storage_path = 'storage/prometheus'\n\
    \      sys.path.append(storage_path)\n      try:\n          import sota_optimizer # For the hex generator\n          import prometheus_utils\n      except ImportError:\n          return np.array([[0.5, 0.5]] * N_CIRCLES), np.full(N_CIRCLES, 0.01)\n\n      # --- Parameters for Two-Stage Search ---\n      num_prospects = 64\n      num_elites = 8\n      prospect_iters = 200\n      refine_iters = 1000\n      \n      # Use the Hexagonal Grid Seeder\n      hex_seed = sota_optimizer.generate_hexagonal_centers(N_CIRCLES)\n      \n      prospect_args = [(hex_seed, prospect_iters, i) for i in range(num_prospects)]\n\n      pool_size = min(num_prospects, os.cpu_count() or 1)\n      print(f\"Starting Hex-Grid Prospecting with {num_prospects} runs...\")\n      with multiprocessing.Pool(processes=pool_size) as pool:\n          prospect_results = pool.map(run_slsqp_path_v2, prospect_args)\n\n      prospect_results.sort(key=lambda x: x[0], reverse=True)\n      print(f\"Prospecting complete. Best score:\
    \ {prospect_results[0][0]:.8f}\")\n      \n      elite_centers = [res[1] for res in prospect_results[:num_elites] if res[1] is not None]\n      if not elite_centers:\n          print(\"No successful prospects, returning best from that stage.\")\n          if prospect_results[0][2] is not None:\n              return prospect_results[0][1], prometheus_utils.finalize_iterative(prospect_results[0][1], prospect_results[0][2])\n          else:\n              return np.random.rand(N_CIRCLES, 2), np.full(N_CIRCLES, 0.01)\n\n      refine_args = [(centers, refine_iters, i) for i, centers in enumerate(elite_centers)]\n      \n      print(f\"Starting Hex-Grid Refining with {num_elites} elites...\")\n      with multiprocessing.Pool(processes=pool_size) as pool:\n          refine_results = pool.map(run_slsqp_path_v2, refine_args)\n\n      refine_results.sort(key=lambda x: x[0], reverse=True)\n      best_score, best_C, best_R = refine_results[0]\n      \n      print(f\"Refining complete. Internal score:\
    \ {best_score:.8f}\")\n      final_R = prometheus_utils.finalize_iterative(best_C, best_R)\n      print(f\"Final score: {np.sum(final_R):.8f}\")\n      \n      return best_C, final_R\n```"
